 "segments": [
  {
   "start": 0.0,
   "end": 3.76,
   "text": "The Richter scale, which has technically been replaced by the Moment Magnitude scale, measures",
   "avg_logprob": -0.117,
   "no_speech_prob": 0.0
  },
  {
   "start": 3.76,
   "end": 4.8,
   "text": "the strength of an earthquake.",
   "avg_logprob": -0.117,
   "no_speech_prob": 0.0
  },
  {
   "start": 5.28,
   "end": 8.84,
   "text": "Since we usually hear about earthquakes with ratings somewhere between 3 and 9, a lot",
   "avg_logprob": -0.117,
   "no_speech_prob": 0.0
  },
  {
   "start": 8.84,
   "end": 11.84,
   "text": "of people probably think of 10 as the top of the scale and 0 as the bottom.",
   "avg_logprob": -0.117,
   "no_speech_prob": 0.0
  },
  {
   "start": 12.2,
   "end": 14.54,
   "text": "In fact, there is no top or bottom to the scale.",
   "avg_logprob": -0.117,
   "no_speech_prob": 0.0
  },
  {
   "start": 15.04,
   "end": 17.6,
   "text": "But even if 10 isn't the top of the scale, it might as well be.",
   "avg_logprob": -0.117,
   "no_speech_prob": 0.0
  },
  {
   "start": 17.94,
   "end": 21.24,
   "text": "A magnitude 9 earthquake already measurably alters the rotation of the earth.",
   "avg_logprob": -0.117,
   "no_speech_prob": 0.0
  },
  {
   "start": 21.56,
   "end": 24.82,
   "text": "The two magnitude 9 plus earthquakes this century both altered the length of the",
   "avg_logprob": -0.117,
   "no_speech_prob": 0.0
  },
  {
   "start": 24.82,
   "end": 26.96,
   "text": "day by a tiny but measurable fraction of a second.",
   "avg_logprob": -0.117,
   "no_speech_prob": 0.0
  },
  {
   "start": 27.48,
   "end": 31.04,
   "text": "In order to get to magnitude 10, you'd need a ruptured fault line 3 and a half thousand",
   "avg_logprob": -0.124,
   "no_speech_prob": 0.03
  },
  {
   "start": 31.04,
   "end": 35.02,
   "text": "kilometers long, and a magnitude 11 quake would require a fault that went halfway around",
   "avg_logprob": -0.124,
   "no_speech_prob": 0.03
  },
  {
   "start": 35.02,
   "end": 35.4,
   "text": "the world.",
   "avg_logprob": -0.124,
   "no_speech_prob": 0.03
  },
  {
   "start": 35.9,
   "end": 39.84,
   "text": "A magnitude 15 earthquake would be a million times more powerful than that, and involve",
   "avg_logprob": -0.124,
   "no_speech_prob": 0.03
  },
  {
   "start": 39.84,
   "end": 43.52,
   "text": "the release of enough energy to evaporate all the water on earth, though if that happened",
   "avg_logprob": -0.124,
   "no_speech_prob": 0.03
  },
  {
   "start": 43.52,
   "end": 46.16,
   "text": "we could rest easy knowing the earthquake couldn't cause any tsunamis.",
   "avg_logprob": -0.124,
   "no_speech_prob": 0.03
  },
  {
   "start": 46.64,
   "end": 50.56,
   "text": "Going a few steps further, a magnitude 18 earthquake would release nearly 10 to the",
   "avg_logprob": -0.124,
   "no_speech_prob": 0.03
  },
  {
   "start": 50.56,
   "end": 54.32,
   "text": "32 joules of energy, which is roughly the gravitational binding energy of the earth.",
   "avg_logprob": -0.124,
   "no_speech_prob": 0.03
  },
  {
   "start": 54.32,
   "end": 58.36,
   "text": "To put it another way, the Death Star caused a magnitude 18 earthquake on Alderaan.",
   "avg_logprob": -0.125,
   "no_speech_prob": 0.014
  },
  {
   "start": 58.84,
   "end": 62.5,
   "text": "You could in theory talk about a more powerful earthquake on earth, but in practice all it",
   "avg_logprob": -0.125,
   "no_speech_prob": 0.014
  },
  {
   "start": 62.5,
   "end": 65.54,
   "text": "would mean is that the expanding cloud of space debris would be faster and hotter.",
   "avg_logprob": -0.125,
   "no_speech_prob": 0.014
  }
 ],
